WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.279 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Five teams. Everyone plays everyone. Could three of them
improve their combined chance

2
00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:08.594 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
of taking the trophy by fixing just their own matches?

3
00:00:08.594 --> 00:00:13.320 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Even the best fair rule must sometimes let them gain forty
percentage points.

4
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:17.426 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Start with one result. An arrow from A to B means A beat B.

5
00:00:17.426 --> 00:00:22.920 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Each pair gets exactly one arrow. The complete picture
records results, rather than the

6
00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:25.004 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
order in which games were played.

7
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.309 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A rule turns that picture into a champion.

8
00:00:28.309 --> 00:00:32.880 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
It may use a lottery. Here each team gets one fifth of the
chance.

9
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.520 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Five equal pieces make one whole probability, or a hundred
percent.

10
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.637 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
We impose one clear fairness requirement.

11
00:00:40.637 --> 00:00:45.376 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
If somebody beats everybody, that team must win the trophy
for certain.

12
00:00:45.376 --> 00:00:51.316 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
This is called Condorcet consistency. When nobody is
unbeaten, the rule has more freedom.

13
00:00:51.699 --> 00:00:57.198 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Return to the circle. Every team beats the next two
clockwise and loses to the other

14
00:00:57.198 --> 00:01:02.499 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
two. Each has two wins, so there is no unbeaten team to
settle the choice for us.

15
00:01:02.899 --> 00:01:09.334 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Now highlight A, D and E. Together they currently hold three
fifths of the lottery.

16
00:01:09.334 --> 00:01:13.831 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
They are allowed to change only matches inside this group.

17
00:01:13.831 --> 00:01:17.707 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Matches against B and C stay exactly as they were.

18
00:01:18.099 --> 00:01:24.512 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A already beat B and C. If D and E now throw their games
against A, those two arrows

19
00:01:24.512 --> 00:01:31.077 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
reverse. Watch A become unbeaten. Fairness forces the rule
to give A the entire trophy

20
00:01:31.077 --> 00:01:31.611 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
chance.

21
00:01:31.999 --> 00:01:34.395 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The group goes from three fifths to one.

22
00:01:34.395 --> 00:01:37.748 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Subtract the starting chance and the gain is two fifths.

23
00:01:37.748 --> 00:01:42.778 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
That means forty percentage points, measured for the group
together, rather than for

24
00:01:42.778 --> 00:01:44.215 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
every member separately.

25
00:01:44.599 --> 00:01:48.966 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Could an uneven lottery avoid this? Perhaps give A almost
nothing.

26
00:01:48.966 --> 00:01:54.655 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The difficulty is that every team has its own version of
this trick: join with the two

27
00:01:54.655 --> 00:01:57.367 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
teams that beat you, and become unbeaten.

28
00:01:57.766 --> 00:02:00.625 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Write those five groups underneath the teams.

29
00:02:00.625 --> 00:02:04.881 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A appears in three groups. So does B, and so does every
other team.

30
00:02:04.881 --> 00:02:10.534 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Whatever probabilities the rule chooses, each probability is
counted exactly three times.

31
00:02:10.933 --> 00:02:15.955 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The team probabilities add to one, so the five starting
group chances add to three.

32
00:02:15.955 --> 00:02:19.223 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Each group can separately cheat its way to chance one.

33
00:02:19.223 --> 00:02:23.701 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Their five possible gains therefore add to five minus three,
which is two.

34
00:02:24.099 --> 00:02:28.549 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Five numbers adding to two cannot all be smaller than two
fifths.

35
00:02:28.549 --> 00:02:34.711 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
At least one group gains that much. This is the known
lower-bound argument from Schneider,

36
00:02:34.711 --> 00:02:38.955 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Schvartzman and Weinberg. No choice of a fair rule escapes
it.

37
00:02:39.333 --> 00:02:42.016 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
But a lower bound is only half the answer.

38
00:02:42.016 --> 00:02:45.338 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
We also need a rule that never allows a bigger gain.

39
00:02:45.338 --> 00:02:48.852 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
One nasty tournament anywhere could ruin the guarantee.

40
00:02:48.852 --> 00:02:52.941 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
So we must control the worst cheat across every possible
result.

41
00:02:53.333 --> 00:02:58.160 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
First remove duplicate work. Rename the five teams without
changing any arrows.

42
00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:03.292 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The shape is the same. We can average over names without
worsening the guarantee, so

43
00:03:03.292 --> 00:03:05.981 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
it is enough to store one lottery per shape.

44
00:03:06.366 --> 00:03:08.781 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Five teams have twelve different shapes.

45
00:03:08.781 --> 00:03:12.886 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Here are the twelve representatives in the actual small
certificate.

46
00:03:12.886 --> 00:03:18.138 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The rule is a lookup table: recognise the shape, read its
fractions, then transfer them

47
00:03:18.138 --> 00:03:20.070 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
back to the original team names.

48
00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:26.761 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Take this real row. The five chances are two fifths, three
tenths, one fifth, one tenth,

49
00:03:26.761 --> 00:03:32.769 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
and zero. They add to one. The table contains different rows
because different match

50
00:03:32.769 --> 00:03:35.130 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
results need different lotteries.

51
00:03:35.499 --> 00:03:38.287 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
For every allowed cheat, compare two rows.

52
00:03:38.287 --> 00:03:44.328 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Add the cheating group's chances after the change, subtract
its chances before, and require

53
00:03:44.328 --> 00:03:47.249 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
the answer to be at most our proposed limit.

54
00:03:47.249 --> 00:03:49.107 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
This creates one inequality.

55
00:03:49.499 --> 00:03:55.178 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Do that for every group of two or three, and every way to
change its internal matches.

56
00:03:55.178 --> 00:04:00.790 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Groups of two matter too. The guarantee covers groups of at
most three; checking only

57
00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:03.563 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
groups of exactly three would leave a gap.

58
00:04:03.933 --> 00:04:08.495 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Imagine each inequality cutting away forbidden choices of
probabilities.

59
00:04:08.495 --> 00:04:11.663 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A linear program searches the choices that remain.

60
00:04:11.663 --> 00:04:17.049 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
We ask whether all constraints can fit at two fifths, the
limit already forced by the

61
00:04:17.049 --> 00:04:17.493 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
circle.

62
00:04:17.866 --> 00:04:23.662 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The numerical solver suggests yes. But a decimal close to
two fifths is not an exact

63
00:04:23.662 --> 00:04:28.768 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
guarantee. A tiny rounding error could put a constraint on
the wrong side.

64
00:04:28.768 --> 00:04:32.218 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The final rule must be a table of exact fractions.

65
00:04:32.599 --> 00:04:38.107 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The discovery process rounds to fractions, repairs the
constraints that must be equalities,

66
00:04:38.107 --> 00:04:43.554 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
and checks everything again. In the plain fair-rule
certificates, only the known five-team

67
00:04:43.554 --> 00:04:46.519 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
circle configurations reach the worst-case limit.

68
00:04:46.899 --> 00:04:50.419 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A separately written checker reads the resulting table.

69
00:04:50.419 --> 00:04:56.371 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
It verifies that every lottery sums to one, that unbeaten
teams win, and that every permitted

70
00:04:56.371 --> 00:05:01.299 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
manipulation stays within the bound. It calculates with
fractions throughout.

71
00:05:01.666 --> 00:05:05.963 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
At eight teams there are six thousand eight hundred and
eighty shapes.

72
00:05:05.963 --> 00:05:11.979 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The checker covers two million, eight hundred and
eighty-nine thousand, six hundred manipulations.

73
00:05:11.979 --> 00:05:16.522 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Its exact largest gain is two fifths. These are recorded
completed checks.

74
00:05:16.899 --> 00:05:19.391 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Why does checking shapes cover named teams?

75
00:05:19.391 --> 00:05:22.230 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The lottery respects every symmetry of its shape.

76
00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:25.011 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Relabelling therefore gives a consistent answer.

77
00:05:25.011 --> 00:05:30.051 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
For up to six teams, a second checker also expands all
labelled tournaments and agrees.

78
00:05:30.433 --> 00:05:34.166 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The same optimal values can satisfy two extra requirements.

79
00:05:34.166 --> 00:05:38.089 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
First, winning one more match never lowers that team's
chance.

80
00:05:38.089 --> 00:05:43.467 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
This is monotonicity. We check it separately; it does not
follow just from being hard

81
00:05:43.467 --> 00:05:44.353 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
to manipulate.

82
00:05:44.733 --> 00:05:47.967 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Second, winners come only from the top cycle.

83
00:05:47.967 --> 00:05:52.927 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Picture a group whose members can reach every team by chains
of wins.

84
00:05:52.927 --> 00:05:55.874 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Teams outside that group get zero chance.

85
00:05:55.874 --> 00:05:59.037 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Here a three-team cycle beats two outsiders.

86
00:05:59.433 --> 00:06:04.785 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The eight-team certificate with both extra requirements
passes the same manipulation

87
00:06:04.785 --> 00:06:09.754 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
checks, plus the extra tests. Across this package, fourteen
certificates pass.

88
00:06:09.754 --> 00:06:15.106 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The recovered eight-team result is an exact certificate, not
an unfinished numerical

89
00:06:15.106 --> 00:06:15.489 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
solve.

90
00:06:15.866 --> 00:06:21.156 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Now put the two halves together. The old argument says no
rule can beat two fifths.

91
00:06:21.156 --> 00:06:25.681 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The new tables achieve two fifths for five, six, seven and
eight teams.

92
00:06:25.681 --> 00:06:29.186 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Matching lower and upper bounds give the exact optimum.

93
00:06:29.566 --> 00:06:34.572 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
With three or four teams, the optimum for groups of at most
three is one third.

94
00:06:34.572 --> 00:06:40.402 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A separate seven-team certificate handles groups of at most
four and reaches three sevenths.

95
00:06:40.402 --> 00:06:44.014 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
These are different finite cases, not one universal rule.

96
00:06:44.399 --> 00:06:48.565 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
For nine or more teams, this work gives no matching upper
bound.

97
00:06:48.565 --> 00:06:52.339 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A table for eight teams does not tell us how to extend it.

98
00:06:52.339 --> 00:06:58.131 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
If three-team collusion ever needs more than two fifths, the
first failure needs at least

99
00:06:58.131 --> 00:06:58.847 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
nine teams.

100
00:06:59.233 --> 00:07:02.319 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Remember three ideas. Arrows describe results.

101
00:07:02.319 --> 00:07:06.143 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A five-team circle forces an unavoidable cheating reward.

102
00:07:06.143 --> 00:07:10.102 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Exact fraction tables meet that reward through eight teams.

103
00:07:10.102 --> 00:07:15.805 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Earlier work already refuted the broader guess for some
large groups; the small-group

104
00:07:15.805 --> 00:07:17.281 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
question remains open.

105
00:07:17.666 --> 00:07:22.831 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The companion paper contains the full definitions, tables
and independent checkers.

106
00:07:22.831 --> 00:07:27.374 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
This animation shows the lower-bound mechanism and the
certificate logic.

107
00:07:27.374 --> 00:07:31.730 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
It does not replace the complete mathematical and
computational proof.
